e is that  none of Alexander's successors none of  these generals were actually related to  Alexander and in antiquity the law of  succession was usually dynastic so you  succeeded somebody who was a member of  your family but none of Alexander's  successors were related to Alexander and  therefore they had to find various ways  to legitimize themselves in the eyes of  their populations and they did this in  various and very interesting ways  one of the ways that Alexander's  successors legitimize themselves in the  eyes of their populations was by having  themselves portrayed as physically  resembling Alexander so if you look at  their portraits whether they're in  statues or whether they're on coins for  example you see that often they are  depicted as having many of the same  characteristics and features as  Alexander's so they somewhat resemble  Alexander of course none of them would  have actually resembled Alexander  because they weren't related to  Alexander by blood but remember that in  antiquity there really was no mass  immediate communication as there is  today and usually most people never saw  the king in real life so the only way  that they knew what the King looked like  would be from an occasional statue that  was set up or perhaps those little coins  that circulated and had the portrait of  the King on him and so if they saw these  these images of the king and the King  looked like Alexander then the King  actually didn't need to say that he was  related to Alexander people would have  drawn their own conclusions so this was  one device for legitimizing themselves  these Kings legitimizing themselves as  Alexander's successors  another way that these that these  generals establish their legitimacy as  Alexander's successors was to imitate  what Alexander had done so they actually  did like Alexander and specifically what  they did was to found Greek cities just  as Alexander had established a city on  the coast of Egypt and named it after  himself Alexandria  so his successors founded new cities  around their kingdoms which they named  after themselves and these were not just  any cities they were specifically Greek  cities what's called a polis these  cities were Greek in that they had Greek  style institutions so for example  theaters for the performance of Greek  plays temples dedicated to Greek gods  council houses for Greek style council  meetings gimnasio so that the youth  could be educated in the Greek manner  and the the reason that they did this  was not just to imitate Alexander but  because these successors of Alexander  used Greek culture as a means of  unifying the diverse populations under  their rule and these populations then  who lived in these cities also grew  loyal to the king because they benefited  from living in a city that had all of  these wonderful institutions so this was  a very effective tool and it is what we  call a a process of hellenization the  making of these empires or kingdoms as  Greek the spreading of Hellenistic  culture or Greek culture throughout the  the realms ruled by Alexander successors  now what I would like to do is to  consider the possible parallels in later  traditions about Alexander the Great and  Jesus Christ these parallels have been  explored by a scholar named Oreo meet I  in a book called from Alexander to Jesus  that was published in 2010 and in this  book I meet I explores the ways in which  Alexander might have served as a model  for the mythical Jesus and noticed the  very important qualification here what  ami Tai is talking about is not the  historical Jesus but the mythical Jesus  by which he means the traditions that  developed about Jesus after his death  on me ties main points are can be sort  of summarized as follows as heir to the  Royal or GAD family Alexander was  conceived as a direct descendant of the  Greek hero  Heracles or Hercules and this was very  common by the way in the Greek and Roman  world that royal families would trace  their ancestry back to a hero or a god  and in this case Alexander's family  traced its ancestry back to the Greek  hero  Heracles or Hercules and Alexander  therefore is often shown in ancient  depictions with the attributes  characteristic of Heracles for example  sometimes he's shown as wearing the  lion's mane cape that you often see  Heraclius wearing omni tie argues that  alexander lived his entire life in  emulation competition and self  identification with Heracles this  established a basis for Alexander's  transformation from human to divine  which was not a feature of the Greek  world before then  now this is before we go on a an  important point because in the Greek  world before the time of Alexander Greek  rulers were not worshipped or identified  as gods as deities this was a  characteristic of the ancient Near East  if you think for example of the Egyptian  pharaohs but in the Greek world until  the time of Alexander rulers were always  very human and they were treated as very  human so one of the new features that we  see introduced into the Greek world  beginning with the time of Alexander is  this idea that the ruler becomes a God  is worshipped as a god this is why by  the way this tradition about the oasis  at sea WA where the priests of the local  or regular shrine of Zeus Emin come out  and Greek greet Alexander as if he's a  god that's the significance of that  tradition so on me ty here is pointing  out the importance of the the claim that  Alexander's family traced their lineage  back to Heracles the importance of this  in Alexander's perceived deification  that is that Alexander becomes a god so  he was descended already from gods and  therefore he himself becomes worshipped  as a God this being new in Greek culture  in addition I meet I points out  Alexander's legacy adopted a number of  motifs from the mythology of Heracles so  not only do we have this sort of divine  Antron seseri going back to Heracles but  there are other aspects of the mythology  of Heracles that become features of  traditions surrounding Alexander  including divine sonship double  paternity a world mission on behalf of  humanity and divinization  now interestingly these motifs are also  a part of the myth of Jesus not the  historical Jesus but the myths that  develop about Jesus  Alexander is a key figure here because  Alexander already was a key figure in  Jewish eschatological thought by  eschatological thought I mean traditions  or expectations among Jews about what  was going to happen at the end of days  in Jewish eschatological thought  Alexander is situated at the beginning  of a new era which is perceived as the  last stage of history before the end of  days the eschaton so in other words  Alexander in in Jewish eschatological  thought becomes connected with  expectations about the end of days and  specifically is thought as being placed  in a in a very key position in an era  right before the end of days is about to  begin  so in other words in Second Temple  period and rabbinic period Judaism  Alexander was understood as a necessary  step on the road to the advent of the  Messiah  now we have already seen that Alexander  plays a key role in the most influential  piece of apocalyptic literature produced  by Second Temple period Judaism  The Book of Daniel according to this  book Daniel stands in the royal court of  three successive kingdoms Babylon which  is symbolized by gold media by silver  and Persia by bronze and he looks into  the future where he sees the progress of  human history in a series of distinct  visions presented as a succession of  kingdoms  the last Kingdom the fourth Kingdom is  Macedonia symbolized by iron and this  kingdom significantly is to be followed  by God's kingdom  thus the Hellenistic age inaugurated by  Alexander is perceived as the last stage  in history before the dawn of God's  kingdom  the perception of Alexander as the  instigator of the last stage in history  before the messianic era based on his  role in the Book of Daniel was to become  a staple in Jewish eschatology in Jewish  traditions about the end of days  Josephus's story about Alexander and the  high priest which we looked at  legitimizes Alexander and his campaign  by making them part of God's plan as  foretold in Daniel so we can see the  influence of the Book of Daniel on later  Jewish writers and later Jewish  traditions now one of the questions that  ami ty asks is how was it possible for  monotheistic mythology to give birth to  a son of God so we would not expect  monotheists like the Jews this is what  Amita argues - to originate a belief in  a son of God where does this come from  and he proposes actually that Alexander  is the key figure here specifically ami  ty proposes that Alexander's historical  role as the paragon of divinization  combined with his positive reception in  wide circles within the Jewish world and  his pen ultimate position in  contemporary eschatology that he's  placed right before the beginning of the  end of days helped prepare the way for  the acceptance of by Jews of the divine  sonship principle this idea that the son  becomes divine  amita notes also the parallels between  Heracles and Jesus divine sonship double  paternity transition from the human to  the divine but only Alexander not here  Ackley's only Alexander was a  flesh-and-blood person who broke the  barrier between humanity and the divine  now Alexander died at the age of 33 and  another well-known parallel between  Alexander and Jesus is that it seems  that Jesus died also at the age of 33 at  least if we go according to the Gospel  accounts according to Luke chapter 3  verse 23 quote Jesus was about thirty  years old when he began his work end of  quote  which means that Jesus died at the age  of 33 based on John's reference to Jesus  celebrating three Passover's during his  ministry  other parallels between Alexander and  Jesus as we have seen include divine  sonship dual paternity virgin birth  world rule mission of the benefit of  humankind death at a young age before  the mission is complete and divinization  the myth of Jesus signifies the  acceptance of alexander's precedent of  divinization by believers in a  monotheistic faith  so ami ty concludes that Alexander was a  bridge between the worlds of monotheism  and polytheism and I quote Alexander and  Jesus were close neighbors in the  boiling matrix of gods  heroes and demons which characterized  the religious life of later antiquity  end of quote and so we conclude with  possible parallels between later  traditions surrounding Alexander the  Great in Jewish eschatological thought  and traditions and the mythology of  Jesus and his life
 

